   330	# --- what the lock protects is a quiet arm -------------------------------------------
   331	# The lock says "one publication at a time"; it does not say "no trainer is running".
   332	# That is this scan's job, and it has to run UNDER the lock: a scan taken before
   333	# acquisition can go stale in the window between, which is exactly how a recovery could
   334	# promote an older attempt while a newer trainer of the same arm kept writing
   335	# (close review 7, blocker 1). `kill -0` asks the kernel whether a pid still exists; it
   336	# is a probe and sends no signal.
   337	UNRESOLVED_GRACE_S="${EXP11_UNRESOLVED_GRACE_S:-120}"
   338	SCAN_REASON=""
   339	NEWLINE=$'\n'   # a real newline: a verdict that is two lines is not a verdict
   340	CARRIAGE_RETURN=$'\r'
   341	
   342	# THREE answers, never two (close review 11). The grammar lives in
   343	# tools/exp11_pidrecord.py and nowhere else -- this shell does NOT read pid files, since
   344	# Bash command substitution drops NUL bytes (close review 10, blocker 1). But a reader
   345	# that cannot run -- no interpreter, an unimportable module, a crash -- used to be
   346	# indistinguishable from "this file holds no record": both were exit 1. Read as "no
   347	# record", a broken reader makes every pid file in the arm look dead, and a resolution
   348	# then retires the attempt of a registered, running trainer. So the reader now answers
   349	# `record <pid>` or `norecord` on stdout with exit 0, and everything else is UNKNOWN.
   350	#
   351	# pid_record <file>: 0 and the pid on stdout | 1 no record | 2 the reader failed.
   352	#
   353	# The verdict is checked as RAW BYTES, in a file, before anything of it passes through a
   354	# shell expansion: command substitution deletes trailing newlines and NUL bytes, so
   355	# `norecord\n\n` and `norecord\0\n` -- neither of which is the protocol -- arrived here
   356	# as clean verdicts and retired a live trainer's attempt (close review 12, blocker 2).
   357	# Only the digits of an accepted pid cross back, and digits survive intact.
   358	READER_UNKNOWN=2
   359	
   360	# one_line <file>: print the single line <file> holds, or fail. The line is read back
   361	# and reprinted, and the result must equal the file BYTE FOR BYTE -- which is what
   362	# refuses padding, a second line, NULs and trailing junk, none of which survive a shell
   363	# variable intact (close review 12's lesson, applied to every verdict).
   364	one_line() {
   365	    local line="" check="$1.line"
   366	    IFS= read -r line < "$1" || return 1
   367	    printf '%s\n' "$line" > "$check" || return 1
   368	    if cmp -s "$1" "$check"; then printf '%s\n' "$line"; rm -f -- "$check"; return 0; fi
   369	    rm -f -- "$check"
   370	    return 1
   371	}
